{
  "image_name": "0db8dbf84480f1accc040d50c816ba9d.jpg",
  "prompt_text": "This figure summarizes how liver dendritic cells interact with hepatocytes and immune cells in different liver injuries and infections. Across the panels, the central idea is that dendritic cells sense danger signals from damaged or infected hepatocytes through pattern-recognition receptors and then shape either inflammatory, antiviral, or regulatory immune responses.\n\nIn ischaemia–reperfusion injury, stressed cells release signals such as genomic DNA, LPS, and HMGB1 that engage TLR9 and TLR4, leading to cytokine production, while IL-10 appears to limit this response. In NAFLD, free fatty acids activate dendritic cells through TLR2/4 and TLR7/9, promoting inflammatory mediators including TNF and IL-6, again balanced by IL-10.\n\nThe viral panels contrast HBV and HCV/fibrosis. In HBV, infected hepatocytes induce type I interferons, but dendritic-cell antigen presentation and T-cell priming appear impaired, suggesting weak activation of helper T cells. In HCV or fibrosis, dendritic cells show antigen-presenting and co-stimulatory molecules and interact with CD4+ and CD8+ T cells, NK cells, and regulatory T cells. Cytokines such as IL-12, IFNγ, TNF, and IL-2 drive antiviral and cytotoxic responses, whereas PD-L1 and IL-10 indicate immune regulation. These pathways also connect to fibrosis, with MMP9 and immune-cell recruitment linked to tissue remodeling.",
